<% content_for :title, "Shame & Accountability | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-triggers-page addiction-shame-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section
    class="addiction-triggers-hero"
    style="
      --triggers-mobile: url('<%= asset_path("shame_mobile_background.png") %>');
      --triggers-desktop: url('<%= asset_path("shame_desktop_background.png") %>');
    "
  >
    <div class="addiction-triggers-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-triggers-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / SHAME &amp; ACCOUNTABILITY
      </p>

      <h1 class="shame-title">
        <span>SHAME &amp;</span>
        <span>ACCOUNTABILITY.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-triggers-intro">
        OWN YOUR SHIT.<br>
        YOUR WORTH IS STILL INTACT.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / GUILT AND SHAME -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-pink">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">01 / KNOW THE DIFFERENCE</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT YOU DID.<br>
        WHO YOU ARE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <blockquote>
        “Shame is ‘I am bad.’ Guilt is ‘I did something bad.’”
        <br><br>
        — Brené Brown
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        You have done things you regret. Drinking, spending,
        impulsive decisions, and consequences that kept arriving
        after the moment had passed.
      </p>

      <p>
        Looking honestly at that hurts. But recognizing
        a harmful action is different from deciding
        you are fundamentally a piece of shit.
      </p>

      <p class="at-lead">
        Guilt focuses on behavior.
        Shame turns behavior into an identity.
      </p>

      <p>
        You broke a commitment, put someone at risk,
        or spent money you needed. Those are actions
        you can examine. Calling yourself unreliable,
        dangerous, or a fucking failure leaves you
        wanting to hide from the whole situation.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / RESPONSIBILITY -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-yellow">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">02 / ADULT RESPONSIBILITIES</p>

      <h2>
        FEELING BAD<br>
        DOESN’T PAY<br>
        THE BILL.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        Your responsibilities still need your attention.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you owe money, address it.
        If you hurt someone, hear their experience.
        If a pattern keeps creating consequences,
        work on a response.
      </p>

      <p>
        Guilt can help you recognize a gap between
        your behavior and your values. You care about
        honesty, so lying bothers you. You care about
        safety, so a dangerous decision hurts to remember.
      </p>

      <p>
        Listen to what that discomfort identifies,
        then ask what action is available.
        An apology, a repair, a conversation,
        or a safeguard gives the feeling somewhere to go.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        WHAT NEEDS YOUR ATTENTION?<br>
        WHAT CAN YOU DO TODAY?
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / ACCURATE RESPONSIBILITY -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-purple">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">03 / WHAT IS ACTUALLY YOURS?</p>

      <h2>
        OWN YOUR PART.<br>
        CHECK THE<br>
        REST.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        Examine guilt before accepting every accusation
        your mind makes. What part was yours?
        What was outside your control?
      </p>

      <p>
        You can take responsibility for a harmful action
        without claiming responsibility for every outcome
        around it. Accuracy matters more than finding
        another way to blame yourself.
      </p>

      <p>
        You do not owe an apology for having bipolar disorder,
        needing treatment, struggling through months
        of depression, or requiring practical help.
        Those experiences need care.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        ILLNESS AND LIMITATIONS<br>
        ARE NOT CHARACTER FLAWS.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Your diagnoses still belong in an honest account.
        Episodes can affect judgment and control.
        Take that seriously enough to prepare,
        discuss it openly, and accept support.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / HONESTY AND SUPPORT -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-black">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">04 / STOP HIDING THE PROBLEM</p>

      <h2>
        SAY THE<br>
        DIFFICULT<br>
        PART.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        You already take your medication.
        Responsibility also includes telling your treatment
        team what is happening, using your safeguards,
        and letting someone know when you begin to struggle.
      </p>

      <p>
        Shame may tell you that admitting a problem
        confirms everyone’s worst opinion of you.
        You avoid your finances, minimize a craving,
        or keep a decision secret.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask what secrecy is costing you.
        The problem continues while you are alone
        managing how embarrassing it feels.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        “I SPENT MONEY I NEEDED,<br>
        AND I NEED HELP WITH THE NEXT STEP.”
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Choose someone trustworthy.
        You do not need a polished explanation.
        Asking clearly is an adult response
        to something that needs support.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / REPAIR -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-pink">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">05 / FOLLOW THROUGH</p>

      <h2>
        SAY IT.<br>
        MEAN IT.<br>
        DO THE WORK.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        When someone has been hurt, acknowledge
        what you did and let them describe its effect.
        Avoid immediately asking them to reassure you.
      </p>

      <p>
        They may need time, distance, or boundaries.
        Your remorse does not obligate them
        to forgive you on your schedule.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then follow through. Return what can be returned.
        Address the bill. Keep the appointment.
        Change the arrangement that made the behavior
        easier to repeat.
      </p>

      <p class="at-lead">
        Make commitments you can realistically honor.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you cannot follow through, communicate.
        Disappearing leaves someone else trying
        to work out what happened.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / SELF-PUNISHMENT -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-yellow">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">06 / GET OFF THE WITNESS STAND</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT HAS<br>
        PUNISHING<br>
        YOURSELF<br>
        CHANGED?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        You can spend an entire evening replaying a decision,
        calling yourself names, and imagining
        what everyone thinks.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask what changed during those hours.
        Did you understand something, make a repair,
        or seek help? Or did you exhaust yourself
        while the practical problem remained untouched?
      </p>

      <p>
        Perhaps a critical part of you believes cruelty
        is keeping you in line. Listen to its fear:
        another drink, another impulsive decision,
        another consequence.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        GIVE THAT CONCERN A PRACTICAL JOB.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Notice warning signs. Review your plan.
        Ask for support earlier.
        You can take something seriously
        without attacking your entire worth.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / LIVING WITH REGRET -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-purple">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">07 / WHAT CANNOT BE UNDONE</p>

      <h2>
        REGRET CAN<br>
        REMAIN.<br>
        KEEP LIVING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        After you address what you can,
        guilt may still remain. You may wish you could
        undo something that cannot be undone.
      </p>

      <p>
        Allow room for grief without assuming
        it requires endless repayment through suffering.
        Some consequences take time to live with.
        You can keep caring for yourself while they hurt.
      </p>

      <p>
        Shame can become another reason to want alcohol
        or weed. You feel bad, want escape,
        and consider something that may leave you
        with more consequences.
      </p>

      <p class="at-lead">
        Tell someone when that sequence starts.
      </p>

      <p>
        You deserve help before you have resolved
        every feeling about yourself.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="at-ending-small">
        YOUR PRESENT REQUIRES ACTION.
      </p>

      <h2>
        OWN IT.<br>
        REPAIR IT.<br>
        <span>KEEP GOING.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="at-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Your past deserves an honest account.
        Your future deserves more than the assumption
        that you will always repeat it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Look for evidence in the commitments you keep,
        the truths you tell, the limits you respect,
        and the support you accept.
      </p>

      <p>
        Own what is yours. Repair what you can.
        Be honest about what exceeds your capacity
        and ask for help.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can feel guilty about an action
        without making shame your identity.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "at-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>